Q: What is the correct way to set SecurityContextHolderStrategy in spring security? A: <p>There is no need to set preferred Strategy globally, via <code>SecurityContextHolder</code> , if it is necessary to use custom Strategy. Since Spring Security 5.8 <code>SecurityContextHolderFilter</code> implements <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/api/java/org/springframework/security/web/context/SecurityContextHolderFilter.html#setSecurityContextHolderStrategy(org.springframework.security.core.context.SecurityContextHolderStrategy)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Strategy setter</a>. And from the very beginning Spring Security XML configuration allows <code>security-context-holder-strategy-ref</code> attribute in <code>http</code> element. But this kind of customization applies to Security Filtering and therefore to any Service, which appears to be upstack of the Security Filters, uniformly, with no distinction.</p>
<p>Custom Strategy <em>per Service</em> is really rare, but let's assume that it is exactly what should be implemented.</p>
<p>Let's consider the following design:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>custom Combiner Strategy, which encapsulates all custom Strategies necessary for each Service and switches between them at the time of invocation of a Service; this Combiner Strategy should be set globally, once per JVM by setting <code>spring.security.strategy</code> System property or anyhow else.</p>
</li>
<li><p>custom AOP aspect and annotation; the aspect determines which particular Strategy is needed for a Service method, annotated with the custom annotation and informs the Combiner Strategy about this choice.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The Combiner Strategy:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Component
public class CombinedSecurityContextHolderStrategy implements SecurityContextHolderStrategy {

    private final Map&lt;String, SecurityContextHolderStrategy&gt; strategies = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    private final static ThreadLocal&lt;SecurityContextHolderStrategy&gt; currentStrategy = new ThreadLocal&lt;&gt;();

    public void setDefaultStrategy(SecurityContextHolderStrategy defaultStrategy) {
    // assumes no concurrent access here, nor any addition to `strategies` later
        strategies.put(&quot;default&quot;, defaultStrategy);
        strategies.put(null, defaultStrategy);
    // TODO add custom Strategies here, e.g.:
        strategies.put(&quot;scopedValue&quot;, new ScopedSecurityContextHolderStrategy());
    }

    @Override
    public SecurityContext getContext() {
        return determineCurrentStrategy().getContext();
    }

    // TODO implement other methods similarly

    public void setCurrentStrategy(String strategyName) {
        finalSecurityContextHolderStrategy currentStrategy = strategies.get(strategyName);
        Assert.notNull(currentStrategy, &quot;Strategy Name &quot; + strategyName + &quot; is undefined&quot;);
        CombinedSecurityContextHolderStrategy.currentStrategy.set(currentStrategy);
    }

    private SecurityContextHolderStrategy determineCurrentStrategy() {
        final SecurityContextHolderStrategy currentStrategy = 
            CombinedSecurityContextHolderStrategy.currentStrategy.get();
        return currentStrategy != null ? currentStrategy : strategies.get(&quot;default&quot;);
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>The custom annotation, for simplicity, applies to methods  only:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Retention(RUNTIME)
@Target(METHOD)
public @interface SecurityContextStrategy {
    String name();
}
</code></pre>
<p>The AOP aspect:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Aspect
@Component
public class SecurityContextStrategyAspect implements Ordered {

    @Autowired
    private CombinedSecurityContextHolderStrategy strategy;

    @Around(value = &quot;@annotation(strategyAnno)&quot;)
    public Object handle(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, SecurityContextStrategy strategyAnno) throws Throwable {  
        strategy.setCurrentStrategy(strategyAnno.name());
        try {
            return pjp.proceed(pjp.getArgs());
        } finally {
            strategy.setCurrentStrategy(null);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        // intercept before Security interceptors
        return Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE;
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>Set Combiner Strategy globally:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Configuration
@EnableAspectJAutoProxy // enable Spring AOP
public class CombinedSecurityContextStrategyConfig {

    @Autowired
    private CombinedSecurityContextHolderStrategy strategy;

    @PostConstruct
    private void configure() {
        strategy.setDefaultStrategy(
SecurityContextHolder.getContextHolderStrategy());
        SecurityContextHolder.setContextHolderStrategy(strategy);
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>And, annotate each Service method, which should be run under custom Strategy:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@org.springframework.stereotype.Service
public class Service {

    @Secured(&quot;ROLE_ADMIN&quot;)
    @SecurityContextStrategy(name = &quot;scopedValue&quot;)
    public void protectedAction() {
        //...
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>To avoid the boilerplate code, <code>SecurityContextStrategy</code> could be trivially extended to classes.</p>
<p>It is possible, instead of AOP, to intervene into Spring Security interceptors/advices chain, but that solution is not more advantageous than our plain AOP-based one as these interceptors/advices are, from the standpoint of Spring AOP machinery, nothing more than just another AOP Aspects, even though they are not arranged in a way <code>SecurityContextStrategyAspect</code> is.</p>
<p>Note that <code>CombinedSecurityContextHolderStrategy</code> stores current Strategy in a <code>ThreadLocal</code>, so if a Service runs some code upstack on a different thread, for example, calls <code>@Async</code> method, then proper thread context propagation would be necessary, otherwise this asynchronous code won't be running under current Strategy of this Service, but under default one.</p>
<p>Please let me know a small POC project is desired.</p>

Q: Deprecated APIs should be marked accordingly in the Swagger UI A: <h3>Definition</h3>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class CustomHeaderFilter : IOperationFilter
{
    public void Apply(OpenApiOperation operation, OperationFilterContext context)
    {
        if (operation.Parameters == null)
            operation.Parameters = new List&lt;OpenApiParameter&gt;();

        var apiDescription = context.ApiDescription;
        if (apiDescription.IsDeprecated())
        {
            operation.Deprecated = true;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Use</h3>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen(g =&gt;
{
    g.OperationFilter&lt;CustomHeaderFilter&gt;();
}).AddSwaggerGenNewtonsoftSupport();
</code></pre>
<h3>The interface must have the following</h3>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>[ApiVersion(&quot;1.0&quot;, Deprecated = true)]
public class WeatherForecastController : ControllerBase
</code></pre>
